adjudication.rationale:
In `EventManager.createAllCalendarEvents`, when `event.destinationCalendar` has entries, it loops `for (const destination of event.destinationCalendar)`. For destinations **without** `credentialId`, it executes:

```ts
const destinationCalendarCredentials = this.calendarCredentials.filter(
  (c) => c.type === destination.integration
);
createdEvents = createdEvents.concat(
  await Promise.all(destinationCalendarCredentials.map(async (c) => await createEvent(c, event)))
);
```

If multiple `destination` items share the same `integration` and lack `credentialId`, this block will run once per such destination and will select the same `destinationCalendarCredentials` each time, calling `createEvent` for the same credentials repeatedly—creating duplicate events for those credentials. There is no de-duplication by credential/integration, and `externalId` isn’t passed in this branch either, making duplication even more likely (e.g., defaulting to "primary" for Google).

In `EventManager.ts` the diff changes the check to:
```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  ...
}
```
If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `undefined`, `null`, or an empty array, `mainHostDestinationCalendar` becomes `undefined`. Accessing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will then throw at runtime. Previously the code used optional chaining (`evt.destinationCalendar?.integration`), which would not crash in that case. The crash is therefore a real defect introduced by the diff when location is Google Meet and no destination calendar exists.
